Based on our current theoretical and experimental understanding, adding a magnetic field between Casimir plates to “knock out” a particle and absorb its antiparticle using cryogenic thermionics is not feasible. While the idea is imaginative and explores interesting intersections of quantum vacuum physics and energy recovery, the vacuum fluctuations responsible for the Casimir effect cannot be directly harnessed to produce real particles without an external energy input that would negate any net gain. For extracting energy from vacuum fluctuations, proposals typically focus on dynamic Casimir effects (where accelerated boundaries produce photons) rather than static magnetic fields and thermionic processes.

In short, while it’s an interesting concept to consider an extra control mechanism (an accelerator/brake) based on vacuum effects, the specific idea of using a magnetic field and cryogenic thermionics to harvest particle–antiparticle energy from the Casimir effect does not appear viable with our current physics.

